NNAAMMEE
       pamunlookup - inverse of pamlookup


SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ppaammuunnllooookkuupp --llooookkuuppffiillee==_l_o_o_k_u_p_f_i_l_e _i_n_p_u_t_f_i_l_e

       All  options  can  be abbreviated to their shortest unique prefix.  You
       may use two hyphens instead of one.  You may separate  an  option  name
       and its value with white space instead of an equals sign.


DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppaammuunnllooookkuupp is best described as the inverse of ppaammllooookkuupp (without --bbyy‐‐
       ppllaannee).  For example, the following normally  yields  output  identical
       the input:

           $ cat input.ppm | \
               pamunlookup -lookupfile=map.pam | \
               pamlookup -lookupfile=map.pam \
               > output.ppm


       Specifically,  ppaammuunnllooookkuupp  takes an input image and produces an output
       image of the same width and height in which each tuple is a single num‐
       ber.   That  number  is  the index in a given lookup table of the tuple
       value that is in the same position in the input image.

       You  specify  the  lookup  table  the  same  way   as   for   ppaammllooookkuupp
       ⟨pamlookup.html#lookupimage⟩ .

       Where a tuple in the input image is not in the lookup table, the number
       ppaammuunnllooookkuupp places in the output index image is one  greater  than  the
       highest index in the lookup table.  Accordingly, the maxval of the out‐
       put index image is the size of the lookup table.



   EExxaammppllee
       Here is an example of ppaammuunnllooookkuupp's function.

       Consider an input image consisting of a 3x2 PPM as follows:


       l l l.  red  yellow    red beige     beige     beige

       and a lookup table consisting of a 3x1 PPM image as follows:


       l l l.  red  yellow    beige

       The lookup table above says Index 0 corresponds to the color red, Index
       1  corresponds to yellow, and Index 2 corresponds to beige.  The output
       of ppaammuunnllooookkuupp is the following index image:


       l l l.  0    1    0 2    2    2


   MMiisscceellllaanneeoouuss
       The _i_n_p_u_t_f_i_l_e argument identifies the file containing the index PAM  or
       PNM  image.   --  means Standard Input.  It won't work if both the input
       image file and lookup table file are Standard Input.

       The output index image goes to Standard Output.



OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In addition to the options common to all programs  based  on  libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common  Options  ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩  ), ppaammuunnllooookkuupp recognizes
       the following command line option:



       --llooookkuuppffiillee==_l_o_o_k_u_p_f_i_l_e
              _l_o_o_k_u_p_f_i_l_e names the file that contains the  PAM  or  PNM  image
              that is the lookup table.  This option is mandatory.





